@h1 A website built entirely in Wit h1@

Build a full, self-styled, multi-section landing page as one Wit project — you
own every byte of CSS and JS, with no framework. The whole site is
@code .wit code@ files and one build command.

@h2 Step 1 — The root file h2@

@code site.wit code@ references the theme, the script, and each section, then
emits them in order — the script last so the DOM exists when it runs.

@@@pre
reference ./theme.wit
reference ./script.wit
reference ./parts/nav.wit
reference ./parts/hero.wit

@stylesheet stylesheet@
@page_nav page_nav@
@page_hero page_hero@
@scripts scripts@
pre@@@

@h2 Step 2 — The design system h2@

@code theme.wit code@ holds one definition whose body is a literal
@code style code@ node — the whole stylesheet, custom properties, dark-mode
overrides, and media queries.

@@@pre
#stylesheet
@@style
:root { --bg: #f6f1e7; --accent: #8a2b39; }
:root.dark { --bg: #14110c; }
@media (max-width: 700px) { .hero { padding: 2rem; } }
style@@
stylesheet#
pre@@@

A double-fence literal node (@@@code @@ code@@@) is emitted verbatim inside a
@code style code@ element: resolve and expand never re-parse or interpolate its
body, so raw CSS passes straight through.

@h2 Step 3 — Behaviour h2@

@code script.wit code@ puts the JavaScript in a @strong frozen strong@ literal so
the braces and template syntax pass through untouched.

@@@pre
#scripts
@@@script
var toggle = document.querySelector('.toggle');
if (toggle) {
  toggle.addEventListener('click', function () {
    document.documentElement.classList.toggle('dark');
  });
}
script@@@
scripts#
pre@@@

A triple fence (@@@code @@@ code@@@) freezes @em everything em@ — needed so the
JavaScript braces and any template syntax are not mistaken for Wit. A double
fence still applies interpolation; a triple fence does not.

@h2 Step 4 — The sections h2@

Each file in @code parts/ code@ exposes one definition — @code page_hero code@,
@code page_features code@ — built from core containers (@strong div strong@,
@strong section strong@, @strong nav strong@, @strong header strong@) with class
parameters. Verbatim code samples go in a frozen @code pre code@ node so
Wit-looking source renders as text, not markup.

@h2 Step 5 — Build with your own CSS in charge h2@

@@@pre
wit build site.wit -o site.html --raw
pre@@@

@strong Result. strong@ A self-contained page (verified at 31,327 bytes from the
worked site): your styles from @code theme.wit code@, the assembled sections, and
your script from @code script.wit code@. The theme toggle switches dark and
light. Open the file straight from disk — there is no server and no build step
beyond this command.

@h2 Pitfalls h2@

@ul
@li @strong Use @code --raw code@. strong@ Without it the default theme CSS is
inlined and competes with yours. @code --raw code@ ships only a mechanical reset,
so your rules win. li@
@li @strong Blank line after a leading comment. strong@ A comment as the first
line of a container body, with no blank line after it, collapses the following
blocks into one paragraph. li@
@li @strong Freeze code that contains braces. strong@ Use the triple fence for any JavaScript or code with curly braces or template syntax you don't want touched; the double fence still interpolates. li@
ul@

@h2 Variations h2@

Split the CSS across several literal @code style code@ definitions; add a section
by writing one file, one @code reference code@ line, and one emit line — the same
additive shape as
@a |href /docs/recipes/manuscript-chapters/| a multi-file manuscript a@.

